package com.dilip.carobd

/** Privacy policy, terms and Play-Store disclosures (generated; keep docs/ in sync). */
object LegalText {
    const val VERSION = 3
    const val UPDATED = "27 November 2026"
    val privacy: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Overview" to "Dr. Vittu OBDII Pro (\"the app\") is developed by Dilip Kumar TD (\"the developer\", \"we\"). The app connects to an ELM327 OBD-II adapter in your car and shows live data, trips, fuel and service logs, fault-code diagnosis and road assistance.\n\nThe short version: your data stays on your phone. The app has no user accounts, no advertising, no analytics and no tracking SDKs. The developer does not receive, collect or sell your personal data.\n\nLast updated: 27 November 2026.",
        "Information the app handles" to "• Vehicle data from your OBD adapter: speed, RPM, temperatures, fuel level, fault codes, battery voltage and similar readings; vehicle name, VIN (read from the car or typed by you), registration number, insurance policy details, odometer and service details you enter.\n\n• Vehicle documents you choose to add: photos or PDFs of papers such as the registration certificate, insurance policy, PUC certificate or licence, with their numbers and expiry dates. They are kept only in the app's private storage on your phone and included in backups you make.\n\n• Precise location (GPS): used for trip routes, speed, the road assistant (speed limit, direction, hazards), parking position and the petrol-station location of a fill-up.\n\n• Fuel and receipt data you enter: litres, price, station name, notes and an optional receipt photo.\n\n• Vehicle identity and documents you add: VIN (read from the car or typed), registration number, insurance details and photos/PDFs of papers such as the RC, insurance policy and PUC. They are kept only in the app's private storage and are included in backups you create.\n\n• Bluetooth / Wi-Fi adapter details: the name and address of the OBD adapter you choose, so the app can reconnect.\n\n• App settings, trip history, alerts and diagnosis results created by the app.\n\nAll of this is stored only on your device (in the app's private storage).",
        "How the information is used" to "• To show live gauges, record trips and routes, calculate economy and driving scores.\n\n• To announce speed limits, speed breakers, cameras and school/hospital zones while you drive.\n\n• To read road signs, find lane lines and the vehicle ahead with the optional ADAS camera and Drive vision. Pictures are analysed on the phone and are not saved, recorded or sent. Standard signs (stop, no entry, school, curves and similar) are recognised by a small model that is built into the app and runs offline. Only the small picture of a sign you choose to teach is kept.\n\n• To save a sign map: when a standard sign is recognised, the sign type and its approximate position are saved on the phone so the road assistant can warn you before you reach it next time. No pictures are stored in the sign map. You can clear it on the Signs page, and it is part of your own backup.\n\n• To suggest possible causes of fault codes and maintenance reminders using on-device rules. No data is sent to an AI service.\n\n• To create backups that you control.\n\nWe do not use your data for advertising, profiling, credit decisions or any sale to third parties.",
        "Location and background use" to "If \"Road assistant\" or background watch is on, the app keeps running with a visible notification and uses your location while the engine/adapter is active, even when the screen is off or the app is not on screen. This is needed for speed-limit announcements and trip recording. You can turn it off any time in Settings / Road assistant, or by denying the location permission.",
        "Services the app contacts" to "Some features need the internet. Only the minimum needed is sent, and none of it is sent to the developer:\n\n• OpenStreetMap tile servers – map tiles for the coordinates you view (your IP address and the tile position are visible to them).\n\n• Overpass API (OpenStreetMap) – coordinates near you to find the road speed limit, hazards and the nearest petrol station name.\n\n• Your own MQTT broker / Home Assistant – only if you turn on Home Assistant in Settings. The app then sends live car readings (speed, RPM, temperatures, fuel, battery, odometer, fault-code count) and the car's GPS location to the broker address you enter. The developer never receives it; the broker's owner (usually you) does. The password is kept only on the phone and is left out of backups.\n\nThese services have their own privacy policies. Their data is open data and may be inaccurate.",
        "Backups and cloud" to "• Backup & cloud lets you save one backup file (trips, routes, fuel log, receipts, documents, service, alerts, settings) to a location you pick – on the phone or in your own Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox or similar. The file goes directly to the provider you choose under your account; the developer never sees it. Anyone with access to that file or account can read it, so protect it.\n\n• Android automatic backup may copy app data to your Google account if you have Google backup turned on in Android settings. That is controlled by Google and your phone settings.",
        "Sharing" to "We do not sell or share your data. Data leaves the app only when you choose to: sharing a report or trip, saving a backup, or when the services listed above are contacted. Information may be disclosed if required by law.",
        "Retention and deletion" to "Data stays on your phone until you delete it. The sign map can be cleared on the Signs page. You can delete individual drives, fill-ups, service records, documents and alerts by swiping them left, or delete everything with More → Privacy & legal → Delete all my data. Uninstalling the app removes its local data. Backup files you saved elsewhere must be deleted by you in that location.",
        "Security" to "Data is stored in the app's private storage, protected by Android. Network requests use HTTPS. No method of storage is perfectly secure, and backups you place in cloud storage are protected only by that provider's security.",
        "Your choices and rights" to "You can use the app without granting optional permissions (the related features will not work), export your data using Backup, delete it as described above, and withdraw consent by turning features off or uninstalling. Depending on where you live (for example India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, or the GDPR) you may have rights to access, correct or erase your data. Because the developer holds no copy of your data, you can exercise these directly in the app.",
        "Children" to "The app is meant for drivers and vehicle owners and is not directed at children under 13 (or the age required in your country). We do not knowingly collect children's data.",
        "Changes to this policy" to "If this policy changes, the new version will be shown in the app (More → Privacy & legal) and you may be asked to accept it again. The date at the top shows when it was last updated.",
        "Contact" to "Developer: Dilip Kumar TD.  {CONTACT}"
    )
    val terms: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Acceptance" to "By installing or using Dr. Vittu OBDII Pro you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, do not use the app. Last updated: 27 November 2026.",
        "Licence" to "You get a personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable licence to use the app on your own devices. You may not copy, sell, rent, reverse-engineer (except where the law allows) or remove the developer credit.",
        "Safety – do not use while driving" to "Do not operate or read the app while driving. Set it up before you start and let a passenger use it, or rely only on the voice announcements. You are fully responsible for driving safely and obeying traffic laws.",
        "Diagnostics are a guide only" to "Readings, fault-code explanations, root-cause suggestions, health scores, range and eco estimates and other \"AI\" results are produced by rules and calculations on your phone. They can be wrong or incomplete. Always confirm faults with a qualified mechanic before repairs, and do not ignore warning lights on the dashboard.",
        "Sign recognition is a driver aid, not a safety system" to "The ADAS camera, the full-screen Drive vision (lane lines, vehicle ahead, estimated distance, lane-departure and collision warnings) and the road warnings they give (for example stop, no entry, school, curves) are an aid only. Distance and lane position are estimated from one phone camera and can be wrong; pedestrians are not detected. They give spoken and on-screen warnings; the app never steers, brakes, accelerates or controls the car in any way. The recognition model was trained on German road signs and can miss signs, misread them or see signs that are not there, especially in glare, darkness, rain or at an angle, and some signs in other countries look different. Always watch the road and obey the real signs. Mount the phone firmly and do not touch it while driving.",
        "Speed limits and road data" to "Speed limits, hazards and station names come from OpenStreetMap contributors and may be missing, outdated or wrong. Your car's speedometer and the road signs always take priority. Estimated limits are marked as estimated.",
        "Adapter and vehicle" to "Use a good-quality ELM327 adapter. Leaving an adapter plugged in for long periods or sending commands to a vehicle can in rare cases affect the vehicle's electronics or battery. Features that clear fault codes erase stored data in the ECU; use them knowingly. You use the app and any adapter at your own risk.",
        "Your data" to "You own your data. How it is handled is described in the Privacy Policy.",
        "No warranty" to "The app is provided \"as is\" and \"as available\", without warranties of any kind, to the extent permitted by law.",
        "Limitation of liability" to "To the extent permitted by law, the developer is not liable for any indirect or consequential loss, loss of data, vehicle damage, accidents, fines or repair costs arising from use of the app. Nothing here limits liability that cannot be limited by law.",
        "Third-party services" to "Maps and road data come from third parties (OpenStreetMap contributors, Overpass API). Their own terms apply.",
        "Changes and termination" to "We may update the app and these terms. Continued use after an update means you accept the new terms. You can stop using the app at any time by uninstalling it.",
        "Governing law" to "These terms are governed by the laws of India, without affecting any consumer rights you have under the law of your country.",
        "Contact" to "Developer: Dilip Kumar TD.  {CONTACT}"
    )
    val permissions: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Bluetooth / Nearby devices (BLUETOOTH_SCAN, BLUETOOTH_CONNECT)" to "To find and connect to your OBD adapter. The app does not look for any other devices.",
        "Location (ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION, ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION)" to "Trip routes and speed, road speed-limit assistant, parking, fuel-station location. Android also needs location permission to scan for Bluetooth adapters on older versions.",
        "Foreground service (connected device, location)" to "Keeps the OBD connection, trip recording and road assistant working with a visible notification while you drive.",
        "Notifications" to "Alerts for faults, over-speed, low battery, reminders and the running-service notification.",
        "Internet, network and Wi-Fi state" to "WiFi OBD adapters, map tiles, road data, and cloud backup.",
        "Receive boot completed" to "Optional: restarts background watching after the phone restarts or when the car's Bluetooth connects.",
        "Vibrate and wake lock" to "Haptic alerts and keeping the connection alive during a trip.",
        "Camera / photos" to "Used only by the optional ADAS camera and Drive vision, and only while those screens are open. Camera pictures are processed on the phone and never saved or uploaded; the built-in sign recognition runs offline. A small crop is kept only when you teach a sign, and it is part of your own backup; the sign map keeps only the sign type and position. Receipt photos still use the phone's camera app or your photo picker."
    )
    val dataSafety: List<Pair<String, String>> = listOf(
        "Data safety summary" to "Data collected by the developer: none. Data shared with third parties by the developer: none. No advertising, no analytics, no accounts.\n\nData processed on the device: precise location, camera pictures for the ADAS camera (not stored), sign map (sign type and position), vehicle/OBD readings, VIN and registration number, insurance details, document photos/PDFs, fuel and service logs, receipt photos, adapter name/address, app settings.\n\nNetwork requests for features: coarse/precise coordinates to OpenStreetMap tiles and Overpass (map, road data); data goes to the service, not to the developer.\n\nEncryption in transit: yes (HTTPS). Delete request: delete in the app (swipe, or Delete all my data)."
